---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy · Rēcoup"
  description="Rēcoup collects nothing. All data stays on your iPhone: no accounts, no servers, no analytics, no third parties."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose-page">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="muted effective">
        Effective {site.privacyEffectiveDate} · Applies to the {site.name} iOS app ({site.bundleId})
      </p>

      <h2>What Rēcoup stores, and where</h2>
      <p>
        Rēcoup stores the things you put into it: the cards you add, which benefits you&rsquo;re
        enrolled in, the credits you&rsquo;ve claimed, and your settings. All of it lives in a
        local SQLite database inside the app&rsquo;s private storage on your iPhone. It is never
        transmitted anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
      <p>
        None of your data. Rēcoup has no backend and no account system. The app doesn&rsquo;t
        send your data to us (there is no &ldquo;us&rdquo; server-side) or to anyone else.
        Everything the app shows you is computed on your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app makes one kind of network request: about once a day it downloads the public card
        and benefit catalog from recoupbenefits.app, so values stay current when an issuer changes
        a credit. It&rsquo;s a plain file download, the same file for everyone, and it carries
        nothing about you, your cards, or your credits. Like any website, our host (Cloudflare)
        sees the IP address a request comes from; Rēcoup doesn&rsquo;t log or keep it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you tap &ldquo;Ask for it&rdquo; to request a card the app doesn&rsquo;t cover yet, Rēcoup
        opens a draft in your Mail app. Nothing is sent unless you send it yourself, and then
        it&rsquo;s an ordinary email to us.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics, tracking, and third parties</h2>
      <p>
        Rēcoup contains no analytics, no advertising SDKs, no crash-reporting frameworks, and no
        third-party code that phones home. We don&rsquo;t buy, sell, or share personal data. We
        never have it in the first place. Rēcoup&rsquo;s App Store privacy label is
        <strong>&ldquo;Data Not Collected&rdquo;</strong> because that is literally the design.
      </p>

      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        If you turn on reminders, they are scheduled as local notifications by iOS on your
        device. No push server is involved, and reminder contents never leave your iPhone.
      </p>

      <h2>Backups</h2>
      <p>
        Your Rēcoup data is part of the app&rsquo;s storage, so it may be included in the device
        backups you control, iCloud Backup or a computer backup, like any other app&rsquo;s
        data. Those backups are yours, handled by Apple&rsquo;s systems under Apple&rsquo;s
        terms; Rēcoup has no access to them. Deleting the app deletes its data from your device.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple</h2>
      <p>
        Standard platform mechanics still apply: Apple processes your App Store download, and if
        you have opted in to &ldquo;Share with App Developers&rdquo; in iOS settings, Apple may
        share aggregated, anonymized crash reports with the developer. That sharing is between
        you and Apple, and it does not include the data you&rsquo;ve entered in Rēcoup.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is static and sets no cookies, so there&rsquo;s no cookie banner, and there is
        no advertising, no cross-site tracking, and no fingerprinting. To gauge how many people
        visit, we use Cloudflare Web Analytics, which counts page views in aggregate without
        cookies and without identifying you or following you around the web. Our hosting provider
        processes requests in order to serve the pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one exception is the launch-updates signup: if you enter your email address to hear
        when Rēcoup ships, that address is sent to Buttondown, the email service that delivers
        the update, and is used for nothing else. It&rsquo;s the only form on the site, it&rsquo;s
        entirely optional, and the app itself never asks for your email.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Rēcoup doesn&rsquo;t collect personal information from anyone, including children.</p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If Rēcoup ever changes in a way that affects this policy, we&rsquo;ll update this page
        and the effective date above. The bar is simple: if the app ever started collecting
        anything, this policy would say so plainly, before it happened.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy?
        <a href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}?subject=R%C4%93coup%20privacy`}>Email me</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .effective {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.4em;
  }
</style>
